<p>Hosting buyer education · private prelaunch draft</p>
<h1>Hosting migration checklist: reduce avoidable cutover risks</h1>
<p>A hosting move is not just a change of subscription. DNS records, mail delivery, certificates, backups, background jobs and third-party integrations can fail even if the new website loads correctly. This is a planning guide, <strong>not a vendor endorsement</strong>. Following a checklist does <strong>not guarantee uninterrupted service</strong>. Use authorized staging and backups, and consult your technical operator for production changes.</p>
<section><h2>1. Inventory the complete service</h2>
<ul>
<li>List website domains and subdomains, origin hosts, CDN configuration, registrar access, DNS providers, and all current A, AAAA, CNAME and TXT records.</li>
<li>Record email routing and authentication DNS: MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC. Confirm who owns changes to each record. A web migration must not accidentally interrupt email.</li>
<li>Inventory databases, file uploads, media storage, secrets, scheduled jobs, webhooks, queues, logs and third-party dependencies.</li>
<li>Document current traffic peaks and the actual business hours in every relevant region before choosing a cutover window.</li>
</ul></section>
<section><h2>2. Check contracts, access and cost exposure</h2>
<ul>
<li>Confirm who can authorize the move, which accounts are required, and whether access will be revoked after cutover. Never share passwords in a migration worksheet.</li>
<li>Compare your own quoted base fees, storage, bandwidth, backup retention, data transfer, support, tax, cancellation and overlap costs. Published fee examples are not proof of any provider's present rates.</li>
<li>Include staff time, specialist migration work and a contingency allowance. The <a href="/tools/software-cost-calculator/">subscription cost calculator</a> accepts your own figures but does not automatically calculate labor hours.</li>
<li>Record data location, data retention and deletion requirements for your applicable jurisdiction and customer contracts.</li>
</ul></section>
<section><h2>3. Prepare recoverable backups</h2><p>For user-entered RTO/RPO thresholds and measured drill timings, try the <a href="/tools/backup-restore-drill/">private backup restore drill planner</a>. It cannot perform a backup or approve a cutover.</p>
<ul>
<li>Make authorized backups of the database, uploaded assets, application configuration and critical DNS settings before changing production.</li>
<li>Verify the backup can be restored in a controlled test environment. A successful download is not evidence of recoverability.</li>
<li>Record restore time, missing dependencies, encryption-key ownership and the last known good production state.</li>
<li>Agree who can activate the rollback and what would make rollback unsafe, especially once writes begin on the new system.</li>
</ul></section>
<section><h2>4. Test the destination before switching traffic</h2>
<ul>
<li>Validate the application in staging using synthetic data. Test logins, permissions, forms, TLS certificate issuance, image assets, API dependencies, redirects and error pages.</li>
<li>Check background jobs and outbound email separately. Avoid sending real customer email or payment events from staging.</li>
<li>Verify mobile layout, keyboard accessibility, response times at representative load, and observability for failed requests.</li>
<li>Prepare an authorized DNS cutover procedure. When supported by the DNS provider, plan TTL changes ahead of the move, but do not assume every resolver updates instantly.</li>
</ul></section>
<section><h2>5. Switch with explicit checkpoints</h2>
<ul>
<li>Record the approved freeze or synchronization strategy for new database writes and files. Keep an incident contact channel available.</li>
<li>Change only the DNS and routing entries approved in the runbook; preserve MX and other non-web records unless those services are deliberately being migrated.</li>
<li>Check website routes, certificates, redirects, API callbacks, mail delivery and monitoring from multiple relevant locations after the change.</li>
<li>Keep the old system recoverable for the agreed validation window and do not terminate old hosting merely because one homepage request succeeded.</li>
</ul></section>
<section><h2>6. Document rollback and operational ownership</h2>
<ul>
<li>Define measurable rollback triggers, such as missing records, broken checkout/login, unacceptable error rates or failed recovery checks, before cutover.</li>
<li>Assign a named decision-maker for rollback and a clear handoff for post-migration support, alerts and billing ownership.</li>
<li>Verify post-cutover backups, recovery access, data retention and decommissioning approvals before deleting anything.</li>
<li>Record any regional policy, contractual or data-processing uncertainty as an unresolved blocker rather than inventing a passing result.</li>
</ul></section>
<section aria-labelledby="cutover-gates"><h2 id="cutover-gates">7. Hosting cutover GO/NO-GO gates</h2>
<p>For an interactive, local-only, downloadable version, open the <a href="/tools/hosting-cutover-gates/">hosting cutover gate assessor</a>. Its results never authorize a cutover; even five self-attested passes mean only readiness for a human review.</p>
<p>Copy this checklist into your own controlled project runbook. Mark each row Pass, Fail or Not assessed; record the evidence and accountable owner. Never enter real credentials, customer records or payment details into StackPilot.</p>
<div role="region" aria-label="Hosting cutover checkpoints, scroll horizontally for all columns" tabindex="0" style="overflow-x:auto">
<table>
<caption>Private planning matrix; these are example criteria, not a completed hosting assessment.</caption>
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Gate</th><th scope="col">Example evidence to collect</th><th scope="col">Responsible owner</th><th scope="col">GO/NO-GO decision</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><th scope="row">DNS and email inventory</th><td>Saved DNS snapshot including MX, SPF, DKIM and DMARC</td><td>DNS administrator</td><td>Not assessed</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Backups and restore</th><td>Authorized restore rehearsal and measured recovery time</td><td>Operations owner</td><td>Not assessed</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">TLS, redirects and dependencies</th><td>Staging test record and working callback paths</td><td>Application owner</td><td>Not assessed</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Rollback authority</th><td>Written trigger, decision-maker and accessible old system</td><td>Incident lead</td><td>Not assessed</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row">Data retention and billing</th><td>Approved lifecycle and overlap-cost estimate</td><td>Contract or compliance owner</td><td>Not assessed</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Stop condition:</strong> Any critical gate marked Fail or Not assessed keeps the migration at NO-GO until the appropriate human reviews the evidence. A staged successful test does not authorize a live DNS change.</p>
